{
  "requirement": "Habeas MUST provide built-in definitions named `habeas:<name>`, available in\nevery project without a declaration. Each built-in MUST have fixed text, which\nstates its obligations and is documented in Habeas's reference documentation, and\nMUST state which reference roles may use it. References MUST resolve built-in\nnames alongside project definitions. So that every book stands alone, the book\nMUST render a reference to a built-in as a badge without a link whose hover text\ngives the built-in's full text. Built-ins MUST NOT require or permit evidence\nfiles and MUST NOT appear in the requirements index, `habeas status`, or the\nmissing-evidence check of {cite}`requirements.ci.coverage`. A project definition\nMUST NOT use a full name beginning with `habeas:`; validation MUST report one,\nand the `:` character already makes such names invalid under\n{cite}`requirements.ci.identifiers`. Validation MUST report a reference to a\nbuilt-in in a role the built-in does not permit, or that breaks the built-in's\nstated form.",
  "determination": false,
  "rationale": "**Verdict: false.** The core behaviour is implemented and tested. However, several pieces of code that carry out parts of this requirement have no `{impl}` reference to it. Two obligations also have no direct test.\n\n**What is implemented and tested**\n- `src/builtins.rs` defines `BUILTINS` with `habeas:none`, which permits only `{cite}` and requires a justification.\n  - `Builtin::text` takes the fixed text from the approved requirement quote in `md/requirements/references.md`.\n  - `Builtin::check` rejects a role the built-in does not permit and a reference that breaks its stated form.\n  - The unit test compares the text with the quote in `md/reference.md`, which covers the \"documented in the reference documentation\" obligation.\n- `validate_references` in `src/evidence.rs` resolves built-in names and reports a disallowed role, a bad form, or an unknown `habeas:x`. Built-ins never enter `definitions`. That keeps them out of evidence files, the missing-evidence check, and status.\n- `collect_definitions` rejects project definitions named `habeas:…`. `project_definitions_cannot_use_reserved_built_in_names` covers this, and it also checks that status does not list the name.\n- `reference_badge` and `replace_references` in `src/lib.rs` render an unlinked badge with the built-in's full text as hover text. `fenced_references_render_their_badge_and_body_and_built_ins_are_unlinked` asserts the `title` attribute and that no link is produced.\n- `fenced_references_in_comments_and_built_in_rules_are_validated` in `tests/references.rs` covers a missing fence, an empty justification, a wrong role, and an unknown built-in. It also asserts that no `evidence/habeas:none` error appears.\n\n**Gaps**\n1. **Unmarked implementation.** Code that carries out parts of this requirement lacks `{impl}` to it, which the assessment rules treat as a gap.\n   - `reference_roles` in `src/lib.rs` recognises built-in names in roles (`builtin = kind != Term && is_builtin_name(name)`). This is how references come to resolve built-ins. It is marked only for `references.targets`, `ci.identifiers` and others.\n   - `opening_fence` in `src/fenced.rs` accepts built-in names in fenced references. It is marked only for `targets`, `span` and `fence_lengths`.\n   - `definition_blocks` in `src/lib.rs` has the comment \"A reserved built-in name is recognized so validation can report it\". This is what makes the reserved-name error reachable. It has no `builtin_definitions` mark.\n   - `AssessmentSources::prepare` in `src/authoring/inputs.rs` resolves built-in references in a subject's text and supplies their fixed text. It lacks the mark.\n   - `missing_function_references` in `src/rust_coverage.rs` and `citation_items` in `src/citations.rs` call `builtin.check`. They are marked for other requirements only.\n2. **Missing tests.**\n   - Nothing tests the \"MUST NOT permit evidence files\" obligation. For example, no test places an `evidence/habeas:none.json` file and checks the outcome. The test only asserts that no such error appears.\n   - Nothing tests that built-ins are absent from the requirements index.\n   - Only the rendering of a built-in cited in a source comment or a book fence is covered. Validating a book chapter's reference to a built-in is not tested directly.\n\nNo FIXME comments acknowledge these gaps, so the result is false rather than FIXME.",
  "references": "779150d1f19be3940a4d63aeb40278078e192dbece08f593ee11f182640e48ee",
